Elite universities (MIT, Caltech, Chicago) that went test-optional during COVID found no decline in student academic performance, suggesting the tests were not measuring the qualities that matter most.
The Flynn Effect shows IQ scores rising 3 points per decade globally — an impossibly fast change to represent genuine intelligence; this rate reveals IQ tests measure learned skills, not fixed intelligence.
Executive functions (working memory, cognitive flexibility, inhibition) that actually predict academic and professional success are not tested by any major standardised exam.
